Check frontend design skill

Now let me plan and build this. The brief is a YouTube thumbnail planning studio — a creative production tool. The right vibe is a dark, focused creative workspace: something that feels like a real design tool, not a generic form. Let me build it.

Created a file, read a file

Here's Thumb Studio — completely self-contained, no internet needed. Here's what each section does:

01 — Brief: Type your video title, channel/niche, the headline that goes on the thumbnail, sub-text, and subject. Eight tone pills (Shock, Hype, Curiosity, Calm, Tutorial, Emotional, Dramatic, Comedic) shape how layouts are styled.

02 — Format: Pick your ratio (16:9 YouTube, 9:16 Shorts, 1:1 Square, 4:3 Classic), crop style (tight face, mid-body, wide/env, product, text-only), and subject emphasis (Left, Center, Right, Split). These wire directly into the canvas rendering.

03 — Layouts: Hitting "Generate" draws three distinct canvas-rendered layout cards — Bold Focus (hard diagonal split), Split Canvas (gradient bleed), and Text Dominant (boxed headline). Every field (headline, sub-text, notes) is editable inline and redraws the canvas live. Each card has its own readability score bar with individual checks (word count, character count, caps, sub-text presence, tone match).

Tiny-size strip: The selected card renders at three YouTube-realistic sizes (68px mobile, 90px home, 120px suggested) so you can see exactly what breaks at thumbnail scale before you ever shoot.

04 — Checklist: Generates a full plain-text shot/crop checklist wired to every selection you've made — crop safety bullets, lighting reminders, text placement guides, layout-specific notes, and export specs. One tap to copy to phone.

